# Build context. Everything the image needs is listed in the Dockerfile; the point of this
# file is that nothing else reaches the daemon — most importantly credentials.

# Never. .env.docker holds the database password and names the AWS profile; an image layer is
# permanent and pushable, so a secret that lands in one is a secret that has leaked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.docker.example

# Built on the host, for the host. Copying a macOS venv into a Linux image gives broken
# binaries that shadow the ones pip installs.
.venv/
venv/

# Regenerated inside the image by the web container's start command.
staticfiles/

# Local state, not application code. celerybeat-schedule.db is the one that matters: the
# beat container regenerates its schedule at /tmp on start, and a stale copy of the host's
# baked into the image is state from another machine.
*.db
**/*.db
logs/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.git/
.DS_Store

# Editor and agent scratch. Nothing in the image reads them, and .git in particular is the
# largest thing here after vendor/ — every byte is uploaded to the daemon on every build.
.github/
.claude/
.impeccable/
.idea/
.vscode/

# Kept deliberately, because the build or the running container needs them and an over-broad
# rule above would be easy to write:
#   pyproject.toml, requirements.lock   dependency install, before the source copy
#   vendor/gcs/                         the `ocs` CLI packages; see scripts/vendor_gcs.sh
#   scripts/                            healthcheck.py runs inside the web container
#   apps/, omicshub/, manage.py         the application
#   workflow_manifests/                 the sample workflow manifest
